<h2>Indian psyllium husk (isabgol) for export</h2>
<p>We supply psyllium husk cleaned and graded by purity and swell factor, either as whole husk (flakes) or milled powder. India grows the overwhelming majority of the world's psyllium, almost entirely concentrated in Gujarat (Banaskantha and Mehsana districts) and Rajasthan (Nagaur district).</p>

<p>Harvest runs March to April, with cleaning, dehusking and grading continuing through the following months. Psyllium is a genuinely stable, low-moisture product that stores well, which gives us flexibility on shipment scheduling relative to more perishable lines.</p>

<h2>A note on the trade data for this product</h2>
<p>We'd rather be precise about a limitation than overstate a number. Psyllium moves under HS code 1211.90 internationally, which is a broad classification covering a range of medicinal and perfumery plant materials — not a psyllium-specific code. India's dominant position in world psyllium supply, and psyllium's likely large share of this particular trade line, are well established facts in the industry, but the dollar figures in the table below reflect the full 1211.90 basket, not psyllium exports isolated on their own. We use this data directionally — to identify which markets buy from this broader category — rather than presenting it as a precise psyllium-only figure.</p>

